General comments:
The manuscript presents a platform to synthesise information on marine migratory connectivity and inform marine conservation, and reveals some of the first results of the data compilation. It is well written and structured, and the platform if very timely and of high value to help filling the gap between marine science and conservation policy. I have however a few main comments, mostly related to the clarification of some methodological steps and the interpretation of the results.
Regarding the methods, some key points are presented very vaguely, or not mentioned at all; the methods section is focused on some detailed procedures that could go to supplementary material, while some main points are not explained. First, it should be clarified that the study is describing geographical links, but not quantifying the strength of such links (sensu Webster et al. 2002; TREE; https://doi.org/10.1016/S0169-5347(01)02380-1). The final sentence of the abstract can be misleading, as this study does not quantify the connectivity per se; it provides a first estimate of the scale and scope of the geographical links. I’d suggest changing to “We present a first estimate of the previously unquantified (minimum) scale and extent of migratory connectivity across the world’s oceans.”
Another major point is the lack of definition of what a “site” means; it should be clarified how were the “important sites” identified. In Kot et al, a site is defined as “start and end points for routes”, but that is a very vague definition (it would exclude, e.g. the stopover sites used by many species during their migratory journeys). In many cases, the start and end points can be simply the tagging location and the location of the last position due to battery failure, rather sites than are more intensively used by the animals; in such cases, the results would be driven by sampling procedures, rather than the ecology of the species. This can be particularly problematic if the main aim is to inform conservation management. The authors should provide more details in the methods section (or in supplementary material) about the identification of these sites, and show that the sites that were included in the analyses are, in fact, a sample of the important sites for the species – e.g. spawning/ breeding sites, foraging sites, stopover sites (even if currently incomplete and biased towards some life history stages).
Regarding the results, I think that it should be stated more clearly that what is being presented here is the platform and its (very high) potential to inform marine conservation, but that currently the results are extremely biased towards well-studied regions – and, thus, they should be taken very carefully when considering marine policies; their current value is to inform priorities for future studies, but not necessarily yet priorities for the conservation of most species. On this last point, it would be interesting to estimate the comprehensiveness of the dataset per species/regions.
Minor comments:
Lines 89-90: If the study includes also within-season movements, that means that it is not only about migratory connectivity, but movement connectivity more broadly. Some central place foragers can travel hundreds of kilometres when commuting between breeding and foraging sites (often larger distances than some migrations); why not removing these movements from the dataset? Or, at least, an estimate of the proportion of within-season connections in the entire dataset should be provided;
Lines 98: It is not clear how the “network models” were built (that is not mentioned in the methods sections); at least a basic explanation should be given, as this is important to understand the tool and the results;
Lines 102-109: This paragraph is repeating information that was provided in the previous one;
Lines 111-116: See my main comment regarding the interpretation of the results; the high number of connections in USA and France are very likely linked to the sampling bias; to be able to interpret the results obtained, it would be needed a sensitivity analysis to estimate how representative is the dataset; reference to Fig 1 in line 116 should be removed, as fig 1 only shows results of ectotherms;
Figure 2: These figures are a great representation of the results, but it would be important to provide an estimate of the representativeness of the datasets of each group (e.g. using IUCN range maps); note that a label is missing in the figure of the turtle (“Central America”?);
Line 140 (and also Line 261): List of species – CMS list is not comprehensive regarding marine migrants; if the analyses only include CMS listed species, it should be clarified that the migratory links of many marine species were not considered here;
Lines 140-151: See main comment regarding the interpretation of the results; without providing an estimate of the comprehensiveness of the dataset, it is premature to take any conclusions linked to the ecology of the species, or the threats affecting them; if the global distribution of sea turtles is restricted to tropical and temperate areas, it would be relevant to look to gaps within their range (and there’s no point in comparing it with the seabirds, as we already know that have a much larger distribution); regarding the seabirds, note that temperate/polar species tend to be more migratory than tropical ones (again, it is fundamental to know the representativeness of the dataset to draw any conclusion); reference to the Great Barrier Reef: are most of the seabird species breeding there migratory?;
Line 256: The MICO system (…) summarizes INFORMATION ABOUT marine migration (does not “summarizes marine migration”); in the following sentence, it should be “connectivity generated by…”;
Lines 265-270: See main comments regarding the definition of a site; it should be clarified here how these sites were identified at the first place form the literature review, as defining the geographic location/boundaries of sites is not a trivial task. The definition of sites can have a major impact on the results (and consequently on all the conclusions);
Lines 292-315: I found this explanation a bit confusing; what does “the largest sites” mean (lines 287, 288)? What’s the size of the buffer used to define metasites (line 294)? Were the data projected to a metric system? (distance metrics are given sometimes in km, others in degrees; it should be standardized or explained more clearly in the procedures);
Line 221: Fisheries are also a primary source of mortality for the other groups;
Line 216: The tool was not built to identify stakeholders, so I don’t think that is possible to “quickly identify” stakeholders with it; it can eventually facilitate the search;

